    {
      "name": "Error cases (what good failures look like)",
      "description": "Not busywork - these are exactly the behaviours Parts 1 and 1.5 were built to get right.\nRun them to see the difference between 401 and 403, and to see validation errors that name\nthe field that actually failed.",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "401 - write while signed out",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [{ "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" }],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/spots",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["spots"]
            },
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"name\": \"Should not be created\",\n  \"province\": \"Cartago\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n  \"location\": { \"coordinates\": [-83.85, 9.98] }\n}",
              "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
            },
            "description": "SETUP: run 'Auth > 4. Logout' first.\n\nShows that the API distinguishes 'I do not know who you are' (401) from 'I know you and you\nmay not do this' (403). The client needs that difference: 401 means show a login screen,\n403 means show an error."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": ["pm.test(\"401 Unauthorized\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(401));"]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "403 - write as a normal user",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [{ "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" }],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/spots",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["spots"]
            },
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"name\": \"User submitted spot\",\n  \"province\": \"Cartago\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"easy\",\n  \"location\": { \"coordinates\": [-83.85, 9.98] }\n}",
              "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
            },
            "description": "SETUP: run Register, or Login as an account whose role is still 'user'.\n\nThis is the entire point of the ownership correction. Users explore, visit, vote and review\nspots - they do not create them, because a user-submitted spot cannot be verified as a real\nand reachable place."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": ["pm.test(\"403 Forbidden\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(403));"]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "400 - validation, with per-field details",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [{ "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" }],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/spots",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["spots"]
            },
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"name\": \"Bad Data\",\n  \"province\": \"Atlantis\",\n  \"difficulty\": \"trivial\",\n  \"location\": { \"coordinates\": [-83.85, 9.98] }\n}",
              "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
            },
            "description": "SETUP: must be signed in AS AN ADMIN, otherwise you get 403 before validation ever runs.\n\nTwo bad enum values at once. The response names BOTH in a 'details' object keyed by field,\nwhich is the shape react-hook-form wants so it can attach each message to the right input.\n\nThis used to return 500. Fixed in utils/normalizeError.js."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test(\"400 Bad Request\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(400));",
                  "pm.test(\"names both offending fields\", () => {",
                  "  const d = pm.response.json().details;",
                  "  pm.expect(d).to.have.property('province');",
                  "  pm.expect(d).to.have.property('difficulty');",
                  "});"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "400 - malformed id in the URL",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/spots/not-a-real-id",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["spots", "not-a-real-id"]
            },
            "description": "A string that is not a valid ObjectId. Mongoose throws a CastError, which used to fall\nthrough as a 500. Now correctly 400 - the request was bad, the server was fine."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": ["pm.test(\"400 Bad Request\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(400));"]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "404 - id shaped right, nothing there",
          "request": {
            "method": "GET",
            "header": [],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/spots/000000000000000000000000",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["spots", "000000000000000000000000"]
            },
            "description": "A perfectly valid ObjectId that matches no document. Contrast with the request above:\nmalformed id = 400, well-formed id that finds nothing = 404."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": ["pm.test(\"404 Not Found\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(404));"]
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "409 - email already registered",
          "request": {
            "method": "POST",
            "header": [{ "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" }],
            "url": {
              "raw": "{{baseUrl}}/auth/register",
              "host": ["{{baseUrl}}"],
              "path": ["auth", "register"]
            },
            "body": {
              "mode": "raw",
              "raw": "{\n  \"username\": \"samuel-again\",\n  \"email\": \"samuel@example.com\",\n  \"password\": \"changeme123\"\n}",
              "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
            },
            "description": "SETUP: run '1. Register' first so the email exists.\n\nReturns a clean 'That email is already taken'. It deliberately no longer echoes the raw\nMongoDB error, which used to leak the database name, collection name and index name straight\nto the client. Fixed in utils/normalizeError.js."
          },
          "response": [],
          "event": [
            {
              "listen": "test",
              "script": {
                "type": "text/javascript",
                "exec": [
                  "pm.test(\"409 Conflict\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(409));",
                  "pm.test(\"does not leak driver internals\", () =>",
                  "  pm.expect(pm.response.json().error).to.not.include('E11000'));"
                ]
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
